##  Terraform Script: S3 Bucket with ACL

For current AWS provider versions, use a separate aws_s3_bucket_acl resource.

main.tf
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 6.0"
    }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "my_bucket" {
  bucket = "my-terraform-acl-bucket-12345"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_acl" "my_bucket_acl" {
  depends_on = [aws_s3_bucket_ownership_controls.my_bucket]

  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.my_bucket.id
  acl    = "private"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_ownership_controls" "my_bucket" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.my_bucket.id

  rule {
    object_ownership = "BucketOwnerPreferred"
  }
}
Commands
terraform init
terraform validate
terraform plan
terraform apply

To remove it:

terraform destroy
Important
aws_s3_bucket → creates the S3 bucket.
aws_s3_bucket_ownership_controls → configures object ownership.
aws_s3_bucket_acl → applies the ACL.
acl = "private" → bucket is private.

Note: AWS S3 now commonly uses Bucket owner enforced ownership, which disables ACLs. If ACLs are specifically required, ownership controls must allow ACLs as shown above.
